{"type":"exercise","type_name":"Exercise","number":"","title":"C1","title_html":"C1","html":"<p>Extend <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"thm-glm-deviance\">@thm-glm-deviance</span>(c) to a Poisson\nlog-linear model in which every <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"y_i\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msub><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D466 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: -0.15em;\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D456 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-script></mjx-msub></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>\nis <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"0\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> or <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"1\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>: show that the deviance is again a\nfunction of <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\upbeta}}\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-TeXAtom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mover><mjx-over style=\"padding-bottom: 0.105em; padding-left: 0.265em; margin-bottom: -0.531em;\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" style=\"width: 0; margin-left: -0.25em;\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c5E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-over><mjx-base><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-b\"><mjx-utext style=\"font-size: 88.4%; padding: 0.848em 0 0.226em 0;\" variant=\"bold\">𝛃</mjx-utext></mjx-mi></mjx-base></mjx-mover></mjx-TeXAtom></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">β</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover></mrow></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> alone. Then show\nthat the argument breaks down as soon as some <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"y_i\\ge2\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msub><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D466 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: -0.15em;\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D456 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-script></mjx-msub><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2265\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>, and explain in words what extra\ninformation the larger counts supply. (This is the reason the deviance\nis a serviceable goodness-of-fit statistic for Poisson data with large\nmeans and a useless one for sparse counts.)</p>","file":"ch34-exponential-families-glm/05-deviance-and-inference.html"}